1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
This is part one of Task 6.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
It is the real Grand Stay Hotel portal running on this laptop, with a temporary database.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:23,000
I will use all six features as a guest: register and log in, check room availability by date, request a booking, confirm it, cancel it, and track the status of my bookings.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Watch the red outline: it marks what is clicked.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Feature one, registration.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
A new guest types a full name, an email and a password of at least eight characters, then clicks Create account.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The account is created, the guest is logged in automatically, the name appears in the navigation bar, and a green message confirms it.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The password is stored only as a salted hash, never as plain text.

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Now login.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
I log out, then try a wrong password.

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:08,000
The message says invalid email or password, and it does not say which of the two was wrong, so an attacker cannot find out which emails exist.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Then I type the right password, and the portal says welcome back.

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Login works, and bad credentials are refused.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Feature two, room availability.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
I choose a check in date and a check out date two nights later, and click Check availability.

16
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:34,000
The table shows each of the six rooms as Available or Booked for exactly those dates, with the type and price.

17
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Dates in the past, or a check out before the check in, would be refused with a message.

18
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Feature three, booking request.

19
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,000
I click Book on the first free room.

20
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
The system creates a booking with the status PENDING.

21
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:56,000
It shows the dates and the total price, which is the number of nights times the price per night.

22
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Pending means requested, but not yet confirmed.

23
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Feature four, confirmation.

24
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,000
I click Confirm booking.

25
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
The status changes from PENDING to CONFIRMED and a message says booking confirmed.

26
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
The confirm button disappears, because a booking cannot be confirmed twice.

27
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,000
The allowed changes are fixed: pending to confirmed, and pending or confirmed to cancelled.

28
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Now the most important rule, no double booking.

29
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,000
I log out, register a second guest, Ben, and search the same dates.

30
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The room that Asha booked is now shown as Booked, and there is no Book button for it.

31
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
The system protects this twice: in the code, and with a database trigger.

32
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Then I log Asha back in.

33
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Feature six, status tracking.

34
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Asha makes a second booking for other dates, which is PENDING, then opens My bookings.

35
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:04,000
This page lists only her own bookings, with the room, the dates, the nights and the current status: one CONFIRMED and one PENDING.

36
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Guests no longer need to phone the hotel to ask whether their booking is confirmed.

37
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Feature five, cancellation.

38
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
I open the confirmed booking and click Cancel booking.

39
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
A dialog asks me to confirm, so nothing is cancelled by accident.

40
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
After I accept, the status is CANCELLED.

41
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:35,000
My bookings shows it, and when I search the same dates again, the room is Available again, because cancelled bookings do not block a room.

42
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Finally, the health page.

43
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,000
It reports the status, the environment and the version of the running application.

44
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Later tasks use exactly this address to check that a deployment, or a rollback, put the right version in place.

45
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
All six features work, and the application also identifies itself.

46
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:03,333
This is the Git collaboration part of Task 6.

47
00:04:03,333 --> 00:04:10,333
Two developers, or here two feature branches, were started from the same commit on develop.

48
00:04:10,333 --> 00:04:16,333
One added the hotel contact footer, the other added a footer showing the running release.

49
00:04:16,333 --> 00:04:17,333
Look at this graph.

50
00:04:17,333 --> 00:04:23,333
The two side lines branch from the same point and merge back one after the other.

51
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:26,333
The common starting point is printed below.

52
00:04:30,333 --> 00:04:35,333
When this happened in the project, I saved the unedited terminal output.

53
00:04:35,333 --> 00:04:44,333
Merging the second feature printed conflict in three files: style dot css, base dot html and the integration tests.

54
00:04:44,333 --> 00:04:49,333
Git merged what it could, and stopped, saying the automatic merge failed.

55
00:04:49,333 --> 00:04:54,333
The status lines starting with U U mean unmerged on both sides.

56
00:04:58,333 --> 00:05:06,333
To show that it is real, I reproduce it now in a disposable clone in the temporary folder, so the project is not touched.

57
00:05:06,333 --> 00:05:15,333
I clone the repository, go to the commit where the contact footer was already merged, and try to merge the release footer branch.

58
00:05:15,333 --> 00:05:20,333
Git reports the same three conflicts, exactly as in the saved evidence.

59
00:05:24,333 --> 00:05:28,333
This is how Git shows a conflict inside the file.

60
00:05:28,333 --> 00:05:33,333
The line with seven less than signs and HEAD starts our side, the contact footer.

61
00:05:33,333 --> 00:05:37,333
The line of equals signs separates the two versions.

62
00:05:37,333 --> 00:05:43,333
The line with seven greater than signs ends the other side, the release footer.

63
00:05:43,333 --> 00:05:46,333
Git cannot know which to keep, so a person must decide.

64
00:05:46,333 --> 00:05:48,333
Here both lines are wanted.

65
00:05:52,333 --> 00:05:57,333
This is the real resolved file from the actual merge commit.

66
00:05:57,333 --> 00:06:04,333
Both features were wanted, so the resolution keeps both lines: the contact line first, the release line below.

67
00:06:04,333 --> 00:06:10,333
The style sheet got one combined footer rule, and each line sits on its own row.

68
00:06:10,333 --> 00:06:13,333
Nothing from either developer was lost.

69
00:06:17,333 --> 00:06:20,333
The third conflicted file was the test file.

70
00:06:20,333 --> 00:06:25,333
Both new tests were kept, one for the contact details and one for the release footer.

71
00:06:25,333 --> 00:06:38,333
And the merge commit message documents the whole decision: what was in conflict in each file, what was kept, and that the linter was clean and all eighty nine tests passed before committing.

72
00:06:38,333 --> 00:06:39,333
That is good practice.

73
00:06:43,333 --> 00:06:51,333
Back in the practice clone I abort the merge, which returns it to the state before the merge, then delete the clone.

74
00:06:51,333 --> 00:06:55,333
Then I show that my real project repository is unchanged.

75
00:06:58,333 --> 00:07:00,333
Now the release tags.

76
00:07:00,333 --> 00:07:04,333
A tag is a permanent label on one commit that marks a release.

77
00:07:04,333 --> 00:07:11,333
We have v1.0.0, the first complete version, and v1.1.0, which adds the nights column.

78
00:07:11,333 --> 00:07:19,333
For each tag I print the commit it points to, and the VERSION file as it was inside that tag.

79
00:07:19,333 --> 00:07:23,333
So the tag really contains the released version.

80
00:07:27,333 --> 00:07:30,333
What is the difference between a branch and a tag?

81
00:07:30,333 --> 00:07:36,333
A branch is a moving line of development, it advances with every new commit.

82
00:07:36,333 --> 00:07:39,333
A tag stays fixed on one commit.

83
00:07:39,333 --> 00:07:49,333
Proof: main has moved on eleven commits since the v1.1.0 tag, mostly documentation, but the tag still points to the same release commit.

84
00:07:49,333 --> 00:07:54,333
Git describe prints exactly that: the tag name, eleven commits ahead.
